      "Part II moves from the making of the philosophy to its foundational principles. It examines OBI- ISM as a practical architecture of responsible living, showing how the principles operate in ordinary personal decisions as well as in leadership, institutions, and public life.",
      "The Part develops eight connected disciplines: prudence, honesty, simplicity, justice and fairness, delayed gratification, accountability, leadership without arrogance, and the human side of governance. Through stories, analysis, case studies, and practical application, it argues that these are not merely admirable virtues but operating disciplines that shape trust, resilience, sustainable prosperity, and human dignity.",
      "The reader therefore encounters the core vocabulary of OBI-ISM such as stewardship rather than ownership, truth rather than performance, enough rather than excess, fairness rather than accumulated grievance, long-term investment rather than present consumption, measurement rather than rhetoric, service rather than status, and people rather than statistics."
