      "Research on leadership legacy shows that the most enduring legacies are not monuments but the institutional and cultural changes that survive the leader. Studies on the multiplier effect of leadership demonstrate that leaders who develop and inspire others create impacts far beyond their own direct contributions. Research on leadership sustainability shows that leaders who accept limits and prepare for succession are more effective over time and leave stronger organizations. The concept of Seed and Tree aligns with research on intergenerational transmission of values, showing that values taught by one generation persist in the next, creating lasting legacies. Studies on leadership humility show that humble leaders build organizations that outlast them, while arrogant leaders often see their work undone after they depart. Research on meaningful legacy shows that the most satisfying legacies are not about monuments but about the positive impacts on people's lives, mirroring OBI-ISM's emphasis on contribution over display."
